WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.388
<v Examiner>Exam case 14.

2
00:00:02.388 --> 00:00:04.418
<v Examiner>Red-green colour blindness.

3
00:00:04.868 --> 00:00:08.885
<v Examiner>Simon is colour blind, which affects his ability to tell colours apart,

4
00:00:08.885 --> 00:00:10.356
<v Examiner>most of all red and green.

5
00:00:10.356 --> 00:00:14.260
<v Examiner>He wants to understand why he has it, when neither of his parents do.

6
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.919
<v Examiner>He's also heard that it's more common in males,

7
00:00:16.919 --> 00:00:20.484
<v Examiner>and is wondering if he'll pass it on to any sons he might have.

8
00:00:20.934 --> 00:00:21.588
Two tracks.

9
00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:24.621
Her recorded session talks this whole case through,

10
00:00:24.621 --> 00:00:28.785
and keys her quiz ten out of ten on screen, so these answers are hers.

11
00:00:29.235 --> 00:00:29.928
<v Examiner>Seven marks.

12
00:00:29.928 --> 00:00:31.952
<v Examiner>What is red-green colour blindness?

13
00:00:31.952 --> 00:00:36.056
<v Examiner>Distinguish between cones and rods, and where they're found in the eye.

14
00:00:36.056 --> 00:00:39.640
<v Examiner>Distinguish their functions, using photopic, scotopic, colour,

15
00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.374
<v Examiner>central and peripheral vision.

16
00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:46.461
<v Examiner>And define red-green colour blindness in terms of the inability to observe certain light

17
00:00:46.461 --> 00:00:47.155
<v Examiner>frequencies.

18
00:00:47.605 --> 00:00:52.981
The hook: cones see colour, so red-green colour blindness is faulty red and green cones.

19
00:00:53.431 --> 00:00:58.423
Cones are for colour vision: central vision, in good light, and much more detailed.

20
00:00:58.873 --> 00:01:02.764
Rods are for non-colour vision: black, white, and the grey scale.

21
00:01:02.764 --> 00:01:06.595
They're more sensitive to light, so they work even in dim light.

22
00:01:07.045 --> 00:01:10.452
Where they are: the macula has both cones and rods.

23
00:01:10.452 --> 00:01:12.123
The fovea has only cones.

24
00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:18.799
Red-green colour blindness is a genetic defect, an anomaly rather than a disease,

25
00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:22.090
that causes dysfunction of the cones of the retina.

26
00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:26.735
It affects the L cones, for long wavelength, red light, and the M cones,

27
00:01:26.735 --> 00:01:28.993
for medium wavelength, green light.

28
00:01:28.993 --> 00:01:31.574
So red and green are hard to tell apart.

29
00:01:31.574 --> 00:01:34.671
It can be detected with a colour-blindness test.

30
00:01:35.121 --> 00:01:39.661
And in the question's own words: cones are photopic, for bright light,

31
00:01:39.661 --> 00:01:41.347
colour and central vision.

32
00:01:41.347 --> 00:01:44.979
Rods are scotopic, for dim light, and peripheral vision.

33
00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:47.183
She says good light and dim light;

34
00:01:47.183 --> 00:01:51.399
photopic and scotopic are the question's names for the same idea.

35
00:01:51.849 --> 00:01:52.440
<v Examiner>Once more.

36
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.451
<v Examiner>Rods, cones, and colour blindness.

37
00:01:56.901 --> 00:02:01.894
Cones: colour, central vision, in good light, with more detail: photopic.

38
00:02:01.894 --> 00:02:05.040
Rods: the grey scale, more sensitive to light,

39
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.938
working in dim light and for peripheral vision: scotopic.

40
00:02:08.938 --> 00:02:12.084
The macula has both; the fovea has only cones.

41
00:02:12.084 --> 00:02:17.897
Red-green colour blindness is a genetic defect of the retina's cones: faulty L cones,

42
00:02:17.897 --> 00:02:22.821
for long wavelength red light, and M cones, for medium wavelength green,

43
00:02:22.821 --> 00:02:25.556
so red and green are hard to tell apart.

44
00:02:25.556 --> 00:02:27.950
A colour-blindness test detects it.
